that point. Three of the numbers are named by the clock arithmetic that runs before the switch for every command. The client takes its own time from 0x1000B1BF + 0xB4, subtracts the third-from-last int32, keeps the smallest difference it has ever seen in the static at 0x102EACA4 and adds that to the first of the three; keeping the least-delayed sample is how you turn a server clock reading into a client one, which makes the subtrahend the server&#39;s clock and the other two a time and a length in server terms. Both then go into the script text, which is an LDB format template: ldb.dll&#39;s LDBformat takes the text, Feed takes the duration and then the corrected start, and Dump produces what ExecuteScript runs. Command 3 uses the same two the other way round - 0x10130C96 computes duration minus now plus start, which is the time the effect has left, and 0x10122DC1 hands that to the playfield along with the script and LDBface::ElfHash of the second string; command 1 uses the same hash at 0x10130E37 to take it away again. The last two int32s belong to command 4 alone: 0x10130B75 subtracts them from the first and third components of the player&#39;s own Vehicle_t::GetGlobalPos, each truncated to an integer, squares and sums, and runs the script outright when the result is at or below 0x9C40 - two hundred units squared. Beyond that it divides 0x2625A00 by the squared distance and compares against a millisecond counter modulo 1000, so distant effects thin out rather than stop. No capture contains one, so ScriptMatchesTheLayoutTakenFromTheClient pins the layout rather than checking it against retail; an entry was already here, with six of the ten named, and two of its readings needed correcting: the fifth and sixth int32s are a start time and a length rather than an accumulated total and a count, and the range check in command 4 measures from the player&#39;s own position through GetClientControlDynel, not from the message&#39;s identity. Before this the message had no C#