$known['FormatFeedback'] = @{
    Status='complete'; Direction='server → client'; Length='89'; Evidence='Every byte is identified. The payload is the client''s formatted-string blob rather than plain text - the leading run encodes the template - and the trailing integer says which kind of blob it is. Across 168 captured copies it is 0 for 158 messages carrying a single literal string, and 1 for the ten that carry a template with arguments; every one of those ten is a tradeskill combine result naming the two items used and the item produced. The leading integer is 0 in all 168. All 168 round-trip byte for byte and FormatFeedbackLiteralTextMatchesRetailCaptureByteForByte reproduces one from scratch. Amber from 2026-09-11 and green again on 2026-09-12. The int32 at 29 is zero in all 168 captured copies, and what a non-zero value means was the one thing open. It is a chat category id: GUI.dll registers twenty three of them by name, and Gamecode passes two of the same constants into the same text-output call. The row below carries the table.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,4,'Chat category','0 in all 168 captured copies','The dispatcher at 0x100395BB pushes it, with the kind at the end of the message and the formatted string, into 0x1005AFF0, which for kind 0 passes it as the first argument of the client''s text-output call at 0x10012B6E - the routine that walks whatever is subscribed to the output object''s + 0x17C and hands each subscriber this value and the text. It is a chat category id, and GUI.dll holds the table: twenty three of them are registered there as a name followed by the id, in the shape push &quot;Me Cast Nano&quot;; push 0x42000018; call 0x100841C7. 0x42000001 to 0x4200000C are Me hit by environment, Me hit by nano, Your pet hit by nano, Other hit by nano, You hit other with nano, Me hit by monster, Me hit by player, You hit other, Your pet hit by other, Other hit by other, Me got XP and Me got SK; 0x42000011 to 0x42000018 are Your pet hit by monster, Your misses, Other misses, You gave health, Me got health, Me got nano, You gave nano and Me Cast Nano; 0x4200001A to 0x4200001C are Team Loot Messages, Vicinity Loot Messages and Research. Those are the categories the chat window filters on, so the field says which filter the text answers to. 0x4200000D through 0x42000010 and 0x42000019 are used by GUI.dll''s settings code and never named, which is what a retired category looks like. Gamecode passes two of them itself - 0x100A23FE pushes 0x4200000B, Me got XP, straight into 0x10012B6E, and Me Cast Nano appears at twenty two sites - and of the 77 call sites into that routine the rest pass 0, as does every captured copy of this message, so 0 is the no-category route','proven'),
        @(33,2,'Payload length','byte count of the blob that follows','Int16 length prefix','proven'),
        @(35,0,'Formatted message blob','template run then text','The client formatted-string encoding, not plain text; the leading characters select the template','proven'),
        @('last',4,'Payload kind','0 for a literal string, 1 for a template with arguments','0 in 158 of the 168 captured copies and 1 in the other ten, every one of which is a tradeskill combine result naming the two items used and the item produced','proven')
    )
}

$known['SimpleCharFullUpdate'] = @{
    Status='complete'; Direction='server &rarr; client'; Length='244 for the shortest captured NPC; every field past offset 34 is flag-conditional'; Evidence='Every byte is read and the layout is proven; eleven of them have no meaning yet. The client never sends this message - its WriteSubClass at Gamecode.dll 0x10078010 is a bare "ret $0x4" - so the reader at 0x1007916D is the only source, and the layout below is a transcription of it rather than an inference from captures. Reproduce the listing with: objdump -d --start-address=0x1007916D --stop-address=0x10079830 Gamecode.dll. All 18,530 captured copies now round-trip byte for byte, and SimpleCharFullUpdateNpcMatchesRetailCaptureByteForByte builds a 244-byte quest NPC from nothing and reproduces the retail bytes exactly. Nine fields were missing before that transcription, and the one that mattered was inside the active-nano list: each entry is five int32s, not four. The fifth was being taken for the array header that follows it, which was then not a multiple of 0x3F1 and was silently read as an empty array, so every character with a nano running on it lost the rest of its packet without raising an error. The earlier standing guess about HasExtendedTextures - that it gated four bytes at offset 193 - was wrong in kind: it gates a counted array of forty four byte entries. Two later corrections, both from widening the audit to every capture on disk rather than the recent ones. The bit at 0x08 next to HasHeadMesh was being forced on whenever a head mesh was written, on the strength of a count over 2,331 copies in which HasHeadMesh never appeared without it; over 18,530 copies it appears without it 944 times, so the writer was inventing a byte the sender had not sent, and the bit is now carried through untouched like the other undecided ones. And the four flags that choose a narrow or a wide form for Level, Health, HealthDamage and RunSpeedBase are the sender&#39;s to choose, not a function of the value: fifteen copies put a level of ten or twenty in a short and ten of those put a health of under a hundred in a full int. A writer that picks the width from the value makes those packets a byte or four short and everything after the level wrong. SimpleCharFullUpdateMessage.WireWidths records the four bits as they arrived and the writer honours them; null, which is every message the server originates, means derive as before.'
    Tests='SimpleCharFullUpdateNpcMatchesRetailCaptureByteForByte, SimpleCharFullUpdateWithRunningNanoSurvivesTheRoundTrip, SimpleCharFullUpdateKeepsTheWideFormsTheSenderChose, SimpleCharFullUpdateCatTextureListCostsEightBytesAnEntry, SimpleCharFullUpdateWeaponPairAndParentDynelKeepTheirWidths, WeaponPairSourceKeyIsFourCharactersAndTheKeyIsASkillForInnateAttacks Green again on 2026-09-12; amber from 2026-09-11, and the correction is worth stating plainly: this page opened with "Every byte is identified" while its own table carried eleven rows that are not. Target identity, the NPC value whose width a flag picks, the organisation id, the counted blob the client copies wholesale without parsing, the immune byte, two unknown bytes, the weapon pairs, the trailing Identity list and the two fields behind the second flag word all have a position and no meaning. The layout is as solid as it ever was - 27,468 copies round trip byte for byte and the reader is walked - but green here means a byte has a known meaning, and eleven of them do not. A pass with StatSets.py over the dispatcher on 2026-09-11 named four fields and confirmed two more, all by the same shape - push the value, push the stat id, call the setter at [character + 0xE8] vtable + 0x40. The byte behind flag 0x20000000 is shadowbreed, 532. The int32 inside the second flag word&#39;s first block, which the model called Unknown1, is mechdata, 662 - the same stat MechInfo carries, named by the same route. The two bytes behind 0x800000 and 0x1000000 are monster scale, 360, written into two different maps on the skill subsystem rather than into the stat table. And the pair the model already called AccountFlags and Expansions is confirmed rather than corrected: the client reads one int32 and splits it, low half to stat 389 expansion and high half to stat 660 accountflags, which is exactly the two int16s in the order this model reads them. Two preconditions came out of reading the dispatcher''s opening on 2026-09-11 and neither was written down anywhere. It resolves the message''s own Identity with n3Dynel_t::GetDynel and gives up if that succeeds - the test at 0x10078392 bails when the dynel already exists - so this message creates a character and will not update one that is already there. And it passes the member at + 0x20 to n3Playfield_t::GetPlayfield and gives up if that returns nothing, which makes + 0x20 the playfield id and makes a copy naming a playfield the client has not loaded a no-op. A server that sends one of these for a character the client can already see is sending it into the ground. The target Identity behind flag 0x20 is resolved with GetDynel too, immediately after, which is consistent with the HasFightingTarget name the flag already carries - a fighting target has to be something that exists. And the list behind 0x40000000, which no capture contains, does have a consumer after all: the dispatcher walks it at 0x10078A8F and hands each entry''s two int32s to 0x1004B515 on the subsystem at [character + 0x1B8]. One more thing the round trip cannot see, found the same way. The health damage field has three arms and they do not carry the same quantity. With HasSmallHealthDamage the client reads a byte at 0x100794D5 and stores health minus it at 0x100794E5; with HasSmallHealth instead it reads an int16 and stores it as it stands; with neither, an int32, also as it stands. So the byte form is a delta and the other two are values, and a server that means to say damage of N must send health minus N when it sets that flag and N when it does not. Reading and writing the raw byte round trips either way, which is why 24,903 copies never showed it. The field is left raw deliberately - converting it would make the round trip depend on Health being read correctly first. Sweeping the reader for arithmetic between a read and a store finds one more, and finding it needed the sweep widened - the first pass looked for sub, add, imul and shl and missed a mask. The packed appearance word is split into six fields at 0x1007921B and after: bits 0 to 2, then 3 to 4, 5 to 7, 8 to 9, 10 to 11 and 12 to 16. This model had five, and its Race was value &gt;&gt; 10 - everything above bit 9 rather than the two bits the client takes. The dispatcher settles it: + 0x89 holds (value &gt;&gt; 10) &amp; 3 and goes to stat 89, race, at 0x10078549, while bits 12 to 16 go to + 0x90 and nothing in the dispatcher reads them back. Race is now two bits and the five above it are their own field, which matters on 230 captured copies where that upper field is not zero - on those the old Race reported a number the client never uses. The packed word is one uint32 on the wire and round tripped through the mistake, which is why nothing objected. The small forms - Family, LosHeight, Level, RunSpeed - really are plain widenings. MonsterData is confirmed rather than changed on the same pass: the message + 0xA4 goes into stat 0x167, 359, monsterdata, and only when it is not zero. The last row closed on 2026-09-12, and it closed somewhere none of the earlier passes had looked: the client''s resource database rather than its code. The weapon pair''s fourth int32 is the four-character code of the record that supplied the weapon, and the third is the key it is filed under. They are equal in nine of the twelve captured entries because for an equipped monster weapon those are the same thing; the three that differ are martial arts, dimach and brawl, whose keys are the stat ids 100, 144 and 142. That also explains the one comparison in the walk nobody could account for.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,1,'Format version','0x39 or 0x3A','The reader compares this byte against 0x39 and 0x3A and abandons the packet if it is neither. 0x3A adds the organisation ID field below','proven'),
        @(30,4,'Flags','packet-specific','The flag word every conditional field below is gated on','proven'),
        @(34,4,'Playfield ID','present when flag 0x40','Skipped entirely when the flag is clear','proven'),
        @('var',8,'Parent dynel','present when flag 0x20','The dynel this character is attached to. The reader puts it at the message + 0x24 and the dispatcher at 0x1007835C looks it up with n3Dynel_t::GetDynel, then branches on whether the Identity is zero: zero and the new dynel goes to n3Playfield_t::AddChildDynel with the coordinates and heading at 0x1007840E, non-zero and those same three arguments go to virtual slot 9 of the dynel it found at 0x10078424, which is n3Dynel_t::AddChildDynel - 0x100059F7 in N3.dll, ninth in all three dynel vtables that carry it. So the coordinates are relative to whichever of the two took it. Called a fighting target until 2026-09-11 on the strength of the flag sitting near the one IsUnderAttack gates; the dispatcher never compares it to anything','proven'),
        @('var',12,'Coordinates','XYZ floats','Always present','proven'),
        @('var',16,'Heading','quaternion, XYZW floats','Present when flag 0x200','proven'),
        @('var',4,'Appearance','packed bitfield','The client unpacks six fields out of it at 0x1007921B onward: bits 0-2, 3-4, 5-7, 8-9, 10-11 and 12-16. The last two are the ones that were wrong until 2026-09-11 - bits 10-11 are the race the dispatcher pushes with 0x59 (stat 89) at 0x10078549, and bits 12-16 are a field of their own, stored at the message + 0x90 and pushed with 0x1A7 (stat 423, currentstate) at 0x100780F7. That second push is not in the dispatcher but in the ribosome: this message implements n3DynelRibosome_i as a second base at its own + 0x18, and the fill method at 0x1007803B reads the field as this + 0x78. The state is zero in 9,298 of 9,357 captured copies; the 59 others are 7, 10 and 11, nearly all with flag 0x1 set','proven'),
