        @('var',1,'Monster scale','110, 90 and 100','Read to the message + 0x120 and taken by the dispatcher at 0x10078A19 into key 0x168 - 360, monsterscale - of the map at + 8 of the skill subsystem at [character + 0x1BC]. Three values that read as percentages of normal. The flag it hangs off is still called IsImmune and that name is still unproven','proven'),
        @('var',1,'Monster scale, second map','','The message + 0x121, filed by 0x10078A40 under the same key 0x168 as the byte above but through 0x10064E5C instead of 0x10064EA9 - and those two differ only in which map they write, subsystem + 4 against + 8. One key, two maps, two values: a base and a modified value, which is the pairing N3Msg_GetSkill and N3Msg_SetSkillTmp work on. Which map is the base is not settled','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 20 per entry','Active nanos','X3F1-counted','Five ints per entry: nano identity, one the client reads and discards, then the server time and the expiry it subtracts and adds to reach a local deadline. Gamecode.dll 0x10052084','proven'),
        @('var',8,'Waypoint target identity','present when flag 0x10000','','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 12 per point','Waypoints','present when flag 0x10000','A plain int count, not the X3F1 form every other array here uses, then that many XYZ float triples. The client stops after thirty','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 12 or 20 per entry','Cloth data','X3F1-counted','Place, ID and one more int per entry, and two further ints when the place is positive and the signed short in its high half is positive as well. GameData.dll RVA 0xA661','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 10 per entry','Meshes','X3F1-counted','Position byte, ID, override texture ID, layer byte','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 16 per entry','Weapon pairs','present when flag 0x100','X3F1-counted, four int32s an entry through 0x10065BCD - which stores them at + 0, + 4, + 0xC and + 8, so the third and fourth are swapped between the wire and the record. 0x100798F9 is its only caller and appends each one to a vector through 0x1006BB6F. The dispatcher hands that vector to the WeaponHolder_t at character + 0x1D4 and 0x1006B14C walks it. The walk is a list, so a node holds the record at + 8: its [esi+8] and [esi+0xc] are the two templates, [esi+0x14] is the third int, and [esi+0x10] - the fourth - is never touched. It looks each item up from the two templates and the character''s level and then files it in a map on the holder under the third int as the key, at 0x1006A166. That map is the one AttackInfo reads: its weapon-instance override goes through the combat routine at 0x1006AE3C, which picks the weapon by slot when the override is zero and otherwise looks it up at 0x10068C19 in the map at the holder''s + 0xC - the same map, filled by one packet and read by the other. One captured AttackInfo carries 0x4C455731 in that field, which is one of the codes seen here. The first two int32s are the low and high template of the weapon: across the 77 captured lists they are a consecutive pair in seven of the eight distinct first entries, and items.dat gives 300523 quality 1 and 300524 quality 400. The other two were the last thing open on this page and closed on 2026-09-12, out of the resource database rather than the code. Read big-endian every captured value of the fourth is four printable capitals, and every one names a database record. Nine are monster equippers, with the code stored some fifty bytes after the record''s name and the name spelling the code out - Equip_LeetBite1 carries LEW1, Equip_LeetBite2 LEW2, Equip_KitevultureClaws2 KIW2, Equip_BureaucratpetRightfist BUW1, Equip_SingleBreedMonsterWeapon SIW1, and QKSI, DMXF, DBPW, EPAH and AZUS sit beside Equip_NPEFireFighterWeapon, Equip_PoisonHit, Equip_Bite, Equip_StickToHead and Equip_Arms. The templates confirm the pairing rather than the placement suggesting it: a monster weapon is the two records X_001 and X_400, and those are the ids the same entry carries - LEW1 with 120910 and 120911, LeetBite1_001 and LeetBite1_400; LEW2 with 120913 and 120914; QKSI with 300523 and 300524, NPEFireFighterWeapon_001 and _400. The three codes with no database record are the ones that settle what the field is for. MAAT arrives with 43712 and 144745, which the database names &quot;Martial Arts Item&quot;; DIIT with 42033 and 42032, &quot;Dimach Item b1&quot;; BRAW with 70292 and 70293, &quot;Brawl Item&quot;. Those are the innate attacks every character carries rather than anything an equipper granted, so there is no Equip_ record to hold a code and the server spells one itself - the same way, MAAT, DIIT, BRAW. And those three are the only entries where the third int is not the same value: it carries 100, 144 and 142, the stat ids of MartialArts, Dimach and Brawl. So the third is the key the weapon is filed under, which for an innate attack is its skill, and the fourth is what supplied it. The client''s cmp against 0x64 at 0x1006B2A8 is reading exactly that - 100 is martial arts, the attack a character falls back to','proven'),
        @('var',1,'Shadowbreed','present when flag 0x20000000','The message + 0x123, and the dispatcher at 0x100789F3 pushes it with 0x214 into the stat table at [character + 0xE8] through vtable + 0x40 - the same setter MechInfo and PerkUpdate use. 0x214 is 532, shadowbreed','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 8 per entry','CAT texture swaps','present when flag 0x40000000','X3F1-counted, eight bytes an entry, read with the shared Identity reader - which is why they were taken for Identities. The dispatcher walks the list at 0x10078A8F and passes each entry to VisualCATMesh_t::SetCATTexture(int, unsigned int, TextureLayer, AlphaMode) through 0x1004B515, with the layer fixed at 1 and the alpha mode at 0. SetCATTexture itself - 0x100729D6 in DisplaySystem.dll, into 0x100700E9 - matches the first int against an existing texture entry''s + 0xC and writes the second into its + 0x14, so the first says which texture is being replaced and the second is the replacement. The list is empty in every captured session, so this is read off the client rather than off the wire','proven'),
        @('var',4,'Second flag word','','Bit 1 gates the stat block below, bit 2 the byte after it and bit 4 the int after that. The client tests these against a local rather than anything it stores, so this word exists only to describe the tail','proven'),
        @('var','4 + 8 per pair + 12','Stat block','present when bit 1 of the second flag word','A plain int count, that many (stat, value) int pairs, then one more int and an Identity. The object the client builds is seeded with 1234567890, the stat unset sentinel, so the pairs are stat assignments. Gamecode.dll 0x10009DAD','proven'),
        @('var',1,'Battlestation side','present when bit 2 of the second flag word','The message + 0x314, sign-extended at 0x10078B38 and pushed with 0x29C into the stat table at [character + 0xE8]. 0x29C is 668, battlestationside. The client defaults the member to 2 when it builds one itself, so 2 is neither side','proven'),
        @('var',4,'Pet master','present when bit 4 of the second flag word','The message + 0x318, pushed with 0xC4 at 0x10078B70 into the same table. 0xC4 is 196, petmaster. The dispatcher applies it only when it is not zero, so a zero leaves what the character already had','proven'),
        @('var',1,'Trailing byte','0 in every captured copy','The client keeps it as a boolean, but the byte is written whole','proven')
    )
}


$known['FullCharacter'] = @{
    Status='values'; Direction='server &rarr; client'; Length='1188 for a bare character, up to 3183 across the captures'; Evidence='Every byte is read and all sixteen captured copies round-trip byte for byte, but three things here are known to be wrong and no capture can show it, so this is not green. The reader is FullCharacterIIR_t&#39;s, vtable 0x10161FA8, reader 0x10073DCA, dispatcher 0x10073F78; the write slot at 0x10073D37 is a bare return, so the client never sends this and the reader is the only source for the layout. The reader is a flat run of calls into typed sub-readers, and the dispatcher hands each finished list to a subsystem hanging off the character object - which is what names them, because the exported N3Msg_ getters read the same members back out. Character +0x1B8 is the inventory (N3Msg_GetContainerInventoryList, N3Msg_GetItemProgress), +0x1BC skills (N3Msg_GetSkill, N3Msg_SetSkillTmp), +0x1C0 nano (N3Msg_GetNanoSpellList, N3Msg_GetFormulaProgress), +0x1D8 pets (N3Msg_GetClientPetID, N3Msg_IsMyPetID), +0x1E0 the team (N3Msg_GetTeamMemberList, N3Msg_GetRaidTeamIndex), and the object 0x10058FF8 returns is perks and research (N3Msg_GetNumberOfUsedPerks, N3Msg_GlobalResearchGoals). The uploaded nano list is the one named outright: the dispatcher appends it to the nano subsystem&#39;s +0x14 at 0x10074150 and N3Msg_GetNanoSpellList at 0x100174E7 returns that same +0x14 as a list of int. The four stat lists differ only in how wide their numbers are - two int32 pairs, a byte pair, and a byte with a signed int16 - and all four go through the same three slots on the character&#39;s stat table at +0xE8, skipping any entry whose value is 1234567890, the sentinel an untold stat carries. The three int32-counted lists share one reader, 0x1003BDC0, and its leading int32 is a version: the reader takes it into a local it never looks at and the matching writer at 0x1003BD7F pushes a literal 1 there, which is also what all sixteen captures carry; the same holds one level down, where the element reader 0x1003BD40 drops an int32 the element writer 0x1003BCFA emits as a literal 1. The inventory entries end in a GameData::ACGItem_t, four int32s of which the type holds three - two templates and a level masked to 0x1FF - with the fourth written as a literal zero at 0x1000E9C3 and read into nothing at 0x1000EA01, so it is reserved rather than unknown. What blocks green is the rest. The int32 after the skill map is two flag bits, split at 0x10073E7B: bit 0 says the character is in a team and bit 1 that the team is in a raid, and when bit 0 is set an Identity follows, then either an int32 and six team objects or one team object and no int32 - the dispatcher writes that int32 to +0x38 of the team subsystem at 0x10074166 and pushes the six objects into it at 0x1007417C, six being what a raid is. Every captured copy has both bits clear, so none of it has ever been on the wire here and the model does not implement it. The two lists after the flags are the wrong element type for the same reason: 0x1002BA77 reads one Identity per entry, not four int32s, and 0x100A71AE reads a whole GameData::SpellData_t per entry through GameData&#39;s own stream operator - an empty list of the wrong type is byte for byte an empty list of the right one, and every captured copy leaves both empty. The research list has a second form as well: its element reader at 0x10052D9D takes an int32 that is either a plain id or a marker whose top twenty four bits are all set, and only the marker form carries the two further int32s this model always reads. The three twenty byte lists - skills, perks and nanos - are lock timers, and today&#39;s captures are what say so. All three carry the same record and all three go straight into their own subsystem through the same range-insert at 0x10074898, which divides by sixteen, so the version at the front is not kept in memory. Nothing in the client reads an entry back that could be found, so the two int32s are named from values rather than code. Seven entries are now captured across two of the lists. In the skill list the identity&#39;s type half is zero and its instance is a stat id - 123 firstaid, 140 mapnavigation and 566 gos - and the first int32 against each is 40, 60 and 600, which are the lock times those three skills have always had, the last being the ten minute Grid recharge. The nano list carries type 1, instance 527, and 180 four times over. Across all seven the first int32 is constant for a given id and the second is not, and the second is strictly less than the first every time - 17 and 12 under firstaid&#39;s 40, 372 under gos&#39;s 600, 3 under mapnavigation&#39;s 60, and 29, 86, 139 and 176 under the nanos&#39; 180. So the first is how long the lock lasts and the second how much of it is left. This entry used to say no capture carried an entry in any of the three lists, which stopped being true on 2026-09-11. The team block went in on 2026-09-11 and the packet can be read in a team now. It could not be before: the block is gated on a flag that is zero unless the character is actually in one, no capture had ever had it set, and four FullCharacters from a two-account session that afternoon sat in the audit as an unreadable message id rather than as a decode failure. The reader at 0x10073E7B takes an int32 and splits bits 0 and 1; on bit 0 it reads the team''s Identity, and then either an int32 and six team objects when bit 1 is also set, or one team object when it is not. A team object is an X3F1-counted list read by 0x101261AC, and each member is a 0x30 byte record read by 0x1012634F: an Identity, a counted string, an int32, a byte and two int16s. The captures name two of those. The first int16 is the level - it holds 5, 6 and 7 across four snapshots of two characters levelling together, rising over time and differing between the two at the same moment. The second is the profession: 14 for one and 8 for the other, constant while the level moved, and 14 is Keeper in this model''s own enum against an account that is a Keeper. The other two, the int32 and the byte, are zero throughout. One limitation is worth stating in the model and here: the client extracts bits 0 and 1 and ignores the rest of the int32, while the conditionals key on the whole value being 1 or 3. That is exact for every value the client can tell apart, and would differ only for a bit the client itself ignores. Three of the list element types were checked against their readers on 2026-09-11 and two of them were wrong, in the way an always-empty list hides: an empty list of the wrong element type is byte for byte an empty list of the right one, so the round trip could never say so. Pets is one Identity an entry and was sixteen bytes; it is fixed. Buffs is a whole GameData::SpellData_t and is still four int32s, because that type is the entire spell format subsystem rather than a record - it is named here so that nobody reads the round trip as agreement. The research entries had a real second form, and that one could have bitten: the record is sixteen bytes when its first word is a 0xFFFFFF00 marker and twenty when it is a plain id, and reading the short form for the long one leaves an int32 on the wire and turns every entry after it into nonsense. Both forms are modelled now, which needed one new criterion, NotHasAll - the complement of HasAll, because the branch is on whether the top twenty four bits are all set and HasNone asks a different question. Buffs was fixed the same day. The obstacle was never the record - GameData::SpellData_t is NanoEffect, which this model has had all along and three other messages already carry lists of - it was that those three reach it through hand-written serializers for the whole message, so the type could not be named from a message described by attributes. Registering a serializer for the type itself is enough, because the array serializer resolves its element through the same registry. SpellList still reads its 6,809 effects across fourteen game functions, so nothing that already worked went near it.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,4,'Message version','26 in every captured copy','Read at 0x10073DF1 and compared against the static at 0x101C11EC; a mismatch drops the message','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Inventory count','X3F1','0 to 34 entries across the captures','proven'),
        @('next','32 per entry','Inventory entries','placement, two int16s, an Identity, an ACGItem_t','Read by the shared container reader 0x1002A5DB and handed to the inventory subsystem at 0x10074125','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Uploaded nano count','X3F1','0 to 15 across the captures','proven'),
        @('next','4 per entry','Uploaded nano ids','nano template ids','Appended to the nano subsystem&#39;s +0x14, which N3Msg_GetNanoSpellList returns as a list of int','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Three-byte list count','X3F1','empty in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','3 per entry','Three-byte records','','Read by 0x1002D1A6 into the eight byte holder at character + 0x1CC. Nothing reads that member back: checked on 2026-09-12 with ClassMemberRefs across all five vtables SimpleChar_t owns, which is the check that would have caught a second base seeing it at a shifted offset - the mistake that hid three of SimpleCharFullUpdate''s fields. Checked again on 2026-09-12 outside the vtables as well. Thirty instructions in Gamecode use the displacement 0x1CC and three load it - 0x10058C7E allocates the holder when it is null, 0x1005F84E and 0x1008A4F8 free it. Allocate, reuse and free, and never the contents','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Skill list version','1 in every captured copy','The writer at 0x1003BD7F emits a literal 1 here','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Skill entry count','plain int32, not X3F1','0 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','20 per entry','Skill locks','version 1, a stat id, a lock duration and what is left of it','Appended to the skill subsystem at 0x100741F6. Read back by the client, which is what names the two numbers. 0x1006414F walks the skill subsystem''s vector - the one this list is appended to - sixteen bytes at a time, matches an entry''s + 4 against the stat it was asked about, and returns the entry''s + 0xC. Its caller at 0x10044F9A hands that straight to the lock-message formatter at 0x10064847, which divides by 3600 and 60 and prints the localised text keyed UnableToPerformActionSkill: "Unable to perform action, %s skill is locked, able in %02d:%02d:%02d". So the entry''s + 0xC is seconds remaining, and the Identity''s instance half is what is locked. In memory an entry is sixteen bytes because the range-insert at 0x10074898 drops the version, so + 4 is the Identity''s instance and + 0xC is the last int32 on the wire. The three captured entries are firstaid with 40 seconds, mapnavigation with 60 and gos with 600 - the ten minute Grid recharge, which is what the numbers should be','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Perk list version','1','See the skill list version','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Perk entry count','plain int32','0 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','20 per entry','Perk locks','same record','Appended at 0x1007423D to + 8 of the perk and research subsystem, and read back the same way the skill list is: 0x10052F2E walks the vector at that + 8 sixteen bytes at a time, matches an entry''s + 4 against the perk it was asked about and passes the entry''s + 0xC to the same formatter, which prints UnableToPerformActionPerk - "Unable to perform action %s perk is locked, able in %02d:%02d:%02d". Empty in all 85 captured copies, which is what a character with no perks used looks like','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Nano list version','1','See the skill list version','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Nano entry count','plain int32','0 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','20 per entry','Nano locks','same record','Appended at 0x1007426F to + 0x40 of the nano subsystem - the same twenty byte record as the skill and perk lists, whose two numbers the skill list''s reader names. Four captured entries, all identity 1:527 with a duration of 180 and 29, 86, 139 and 176 seconds left, which is a three minute lock seen four times as it runs down','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Stat list 1 count','X3F1','76 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','8 per stat','Stats, int32 id and int32 value','','All four stat lists go through the same set on the character&#39;s stat table; 1234567890 means skip','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Stat list 2 count','X3F1','146 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','8 per stat','Stats, int32 id and int32 value','','Read by the same 0x1002E7D9 as list 1 and applied identically','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Stat list 3 count','X3F1','8 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','2 per stat','Stats, byte id and byte value','','Element reader 0x1002EC75','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Stat list 4 count','X3F1','14 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','3 per stat','Stats, byte id and signed int16 value','','Element reader 0x1002E905; the dispatcher sign-extends at 0x100740C9','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Skill map count','plain int32, not X3F1','0 on free accounts, 3 on the one subscribed account','proven'),
        @('next','8 per entry','Skill map, key and value','516, 540, 553 and 557 each with a zero','Read by 0x10009E3C. The dispatcher hands the whole map to the skill subsystem at 0x10073FB7, and 0x100646B2 is a plain setter - it frees whatever was at subsystem + 8 and parks this one there. Nothing in the dispatcher reads it back, so the keys are not named by what the client does with them here. The four that appear are stat ids in this model - 516 attackshield, 540 procinitiative2, 553 procnano2, 557 procchance2, a coherent group of a shield and three procs - and on 2026-09-12 the container settled it rather than the values. That map is stat-keyed at both ends. The setter at 0x10064EA9 takes a key and a value and inserts into subsystem + 8, and it is the one SimpleCharFullUpdate''s dispatcher calls with 0x168 - stat 360, monsterscale - to file monster scale. The getter at 0x10064D77 reads the same + 8 with the same key through the map find at 0x10084E6E. Its siblings are the same shape: 0x10064E5C writes subsystem + 4, 0x10064EFD writes + 0x14, and N3Msg_SetSkillTmp - whose signature is (Stat_e, int) - is the export that lands on that one. So a key in any of these maps is a stat id, and this list replaces the whole of + 8','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Team flags','0 solo, 1 in a team','Bit 0 in a team, bit 1 that team is in a raid. Both bits clear until 2026-09-11, when a two-account session produced four copies with bit 0 set - which this model could not read at all until the block behind it was implemented','proven'),
        @('next',8,'Team','only when in a team','The team''s Identity, which in the captured copies equals the first member''s','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Raid team index','only on a raid','Written to + 0x38 of the team subsystem, the member N3Msg_GetRaidTeamIndex reads. No capture contains a raid','proven'),
        @('next','X3F1 list','Team members','two in the captured copies','Identity, name, organisation, side, level and profession each - thirty byte records built by 0x1012634F. The client names the two that had no name: every finished entry goes through the validator at 0x101262E6, and a record that fails it is logged with the format string at 0x101712E0 - player "%s" (%u:%u), level %u, side %u, organisation %u - whose arguments are pushed at 0x1012632B in that order, putting side on the byte at + 0x28 and organisation on the int32 at + 0x24. The validator bounds them the same way: identity type 0xC350, side at most 7, level below 1000, profession below 16. Profession is confirmed from the other end - GUI.dll formats GFX_GUI_ICON_PROFESSION_%d with the record''s + 0x2C at 0x10100E8A. Six of these lists on a raid, one otherwise','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Pet count','X3F1','0 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','8 per entry','Pets','always empty','0x1002BA77 reads one Identity per entry and the dispatcher walks them into the pet subsystem at 0x100742B0, the member N3Msg_GetClientPetID reads back. The model read sixteen bytes an entry until 2026-09-11 - an empty list of the wrong element type is byte for byte an empty list of the right one, so no capture could contradict it','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Buff count','X3F1, refused above 999','0 in every captured copy','proven'),
        @('next','per entry','Buffs','always empty','0x100A71AE reads each entry with GameData&#39;s own operator for SpellData_t. It was four int32s an entry and is now a NanoEffect, which is what SpellData_t already was in this model - ApplySpells, SpellList and CorpseFullUpdate have carried lists of them all along, and SpellFormats.py reads the client&#39;s own format table for the argument counts. Only the plumbing was missing: those three reach it from hand-written serializers, so NanoEffectSerializer registers the type and the array serializer picks it up','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Research count','X3F1, refused above 1000','0 on free accounts, 4 on the one subscribed account','proven'),
        @('next','16 or 20 per entry','Research and perk progress','id, 0xFFFFFF02, the id again, and a zero','Both forms are modelled since 2026-09-11. 0x10052D9D masks the record&#39;s first word with 0xFFFFFF00: all set makes it a marker with a form tag in the low byte and two int32s after it, sixteen bytes in the entry; any bit clear makes it an id with three int32s after it through 0x1002BC4D, twenty. The client keeps an entry only when the id ahead of the record matches the id inside it. This page said until 2026-09-12 that the three int32s are read and dropped, and that is true of the plain form and not of the marker form. 0x1002BC4D reads the plain form&#39;s three into a stack local that nothing touches again, and both forms then converge on 0x10052E1A, which looks the id up and copies the seven word definition over the slot with a rep movsd at 0x10052E3E - so those three really are gone. But the marker form&#39;s second int32 is not one of them: 0x10052DE7 reads it straight into the slot&#39;s + 0x1C, which is the eighth word and sits past the seven the rep movsd overwrites. It survives, and it is read. N3Msg_GetPerkProgress at 0x1002781F looks the same slot up and returns (definition + 0x18 minus slot + 0x1C) divided by definition + 0x18, so the field is the experience still owed on that perk or research line and the definition&#39;s + 0x18 is the total. The one qualification is at 0x10052E2F: when the definition&#39;s flags at + 0xC have neither bit 0x40 nor bit 0x80 the client zeroes the field before using it. This is the same + 0x1C that ResearchUpdate and PerkUpdate write experience remaining into, and FullCharacterEntry has said so since 2026-09-11 - the class had it and the page did not','proven')
    )
}


$known['ClientRequestBuy'] = @{
    Status='complete'; Direction='client &rarr; server'; Length='33'; Evidence='Every byte is identified, and there is one field. One of the five messages the city GUI sends, and all five are built the same way: the sender takes the city&#39;s CityAI_c, calls GetID - exported from city.dll at 0x100027E2, returning a CityID_t - and pushes that structure&#39;s +4 into the message. CityID_t opens with an Identity, read by city.dll&#39;s own identity operator at 0x100274BB, so +4 is its instance half and the int32 on the wire is which city. Nothing more of the city id travels, because the request arrives at that city&#39;s own CityAI_c and the server has the rest. These are messages the client only sends - every one of the five dispatchers does nothing but clear the pass-on flag - and no capture contains one, because catching one would need a city. What this one asks for is settled on the other side: city.dll exports CityAI_c::ClientRequestBuy(Identity const&amp;) at 0x100029E4, which takes the requesting character and nothing else. Extracted-client ClientRequestBuyIIR_c has vtable 0x10172170, reader 0x1013015A, writer 0x1013017A, constructor 0x10130196 and dispatcher 0x1013018D; the reader takes one int32 and the writer puts back the same one. The constructor registers the class name ClientRequestBuyIIR_c, which is what the id hashes from, and it is called from one place - the city GUI sender at 0x1012E5F9. ClientRequestBuyMatchesTheLayoutTakenFromTheClient writes the packet from scratch.'
    Tests='ClientRequestBuyMatchesTheLayoutTakenFromTheClient'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,4,'City','the instance half of the city&#39;s CityID_t','Pushed straight from CityAI_c::GetID at the call site','proven')
    )
}

