$known['TrapItemFullUpdate'] = @{
    Status='values'; Direction='server &rarr; client'; Length='the SimpleItemFullUpdate body plus 21'; Evidence='A SimpleItemFullUpdate with five fields on the end, and the byte that closes it is now two named bits. Extracted-client TrapItemFullUpdateIIR_t has vtable 0x10167E00, reader 0x100A28CA, writer 0x100A2954 and dispatcher 0x100A2A96. The reader calls SimpleItemFullUpdate&#39;s own reader at 0x100A1661 for everything the family shares, then takes an int32 version checked against the static at 0x101C21C4, an int32, two Identities and a byte; the writer emits the static rather than a field for the version and packs the byte from two booleans, so the two agree. The int32 is named by the constructor that builds one of these from a live trap: at 0x100A2827 it reads stat 0x121 from the dynel, and 0x121 is 289, TrapDifficulty. A TrapItem_t is created with 50 in it, at 0x1009937B. The byte was the open question and it is settled. It is one byte and not two, and the reader at 0x10099736 splits it - bit 0 to the message&#39;s + 0x88 and bit 1 to + 0x89 - while the writer packs them back the same way; the object holds them as two adjacent bytes at TrapItem_t + 0x1E5 and + 0x1E4, and the TrapItem_t constructor sets both in one instruction, mov word ptr [esi + 0x1E4], 1, which is what says they are two bytes and not one field. Bit 1, at + 0x1E4, is Armed. CanBeTriggeredBy - TrapItem_t slot 0xF0, at 0x10099716 - returns false at once when it is clear; the disarm helper at 0x10085E9D does nothing unless it is set and clears it when it acts; and the feedback at 0x10085D72 picks between the client&#39;s own two strings on it, &quot;Feedback_MineIsArmed&quot; when set and &quot;Feedback_MineAlreadyDisarmed&quot; when not. A trap is created armed. Bit 0, at + 0x1E5, is Revealed - whether this character has already spotted the trap. It is the rest of CanBeTriggeredBy: past the armed test the routine returns true immediately when this is set, and otherwise takes the character&#39