$known['InfoPacket'] = @{
    Status='complete'; Direction='server → client'; Length='varies with the flags; 532 captured copies'; Evidence='Rewritten on 2026-09-11 as the one flags-driven record the client actually has, and two misplaced fields came out of it. Before that this was an abstract class with three subclasses - character, monster and tower - chosen by matching the first byte against a list of seven whole values: 0x40, 0x41, 0x43, 0x47, 0x50, 0x54 and 0x5C. The client does no such thing. It has one reader, at Gamecode 0x10045F6C, and one structure, and it tests that byte a bit at a time, so any combination of the bits is a legal message. A value outside the list of seven selected no shape at all and the body would not have been read - which is exactly what FullCharacter did with its team block and what Mail did with its whole message, both found the same day by a capture carrying a combination nobody had caught before.<br><br>The reader in full, instruction by instruction, with the record offsets it fills:<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;byte to + 0, the flags<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x40, a byte to + 1, else + 1 is set to zero - the version<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;three bytes to + 0x32, + 0x33, + 0x34<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if the version is zero, one byte read and dropped<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;a byte to + 0x35<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;an int16 to + 0x30<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;four int32s to + 0x20, + 0x24, + 0x28, + 0x2C<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;four counted strings to + 0x48, + 0x64, + 0x80 and + 4<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x01: a string to + 0x9C, then if flags &amp; 0x02 an X3F1 list through 0x1012916A to + 0xB8, then an int32 to + 0x38<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x04: an X3F1 list through 0x10046894 to + 0xCC<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x08: an int32 to + 0xD8 and a byte to + 0xDC<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x20: twelve int32s to + 0xE0 through + 0x10C<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;three int32s to + 0x3C, + 0x40, + 0x44<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;if flags &amp; 0x10 is CLEAR: eight int32s to + 0x110 through + 0x12C<br><br>Two things in that are easy to get wrong and both were wrong here. The int32 at + 0x38 belongs to the 0x01 branch and not to the 0x02 one: the jump for bit 0 clears the whole block including it, while the jump for bit 1 skips only the list and lands on it. And 0x10 suppresses rather than selects - the test at 0x1004618C jumps past the eight int32s when the bit is set.<br><br>The two real defects. The organization id is the fourth of the four unconditional int32s, and the old model made it conditional on the organization bits, so a plain character read three int32s where the client reads four and cut the four counted strings four bytes early. The city playfield id was the mirror of it: unconditional in the model, and only inside the 0x01 branch in the client. The two errors cancelled in the byte count, which is the whole reason every captured copy still round tripped - a plain character came out level again at the end, having named the wrong bytes the whole way through the middle. It worked only while a plain character''s organization id was zero and its four names were empty, which is true of all 50 captured copies of that shape and is not a property of the protocol. Two copies on disk already carry an organization id of 9988 with no organization bit set, in the monster shape, which is what a controlled pet in an organization looks like.<br><br>The rewritten reader was then walked over every copy on disk - 471 out of the captures and the six in TestData, 477 in all - and it ends exactly on the last byte of each. The audit reads 532 and round trips all of them byte for byte. Four flag combinations appear: 0x40 fifty times, 0x41 once, 0x43 once and 0x50 four hundred and eighty. Nothing has ever sent 0x08, 0x20, or a record without 0x40, so the suppression pair, the twelve int32s and the dropped byte are modelled from the reader alone - and on 2026-09-12 the reader turned out to name them. It files twenty-eight of its fields straight into the examined character''s stat table, each push carrying the stat id, which settles health, life, breedhostility, clan, profession, professionlevel, titlelevel, visualprofession, invaderskilled, killedbyinvaders and alienlevel; the twelve int32s go to stats 561 to 572, the faction standings; the eight PvP int32s to 674 to 684. The inherited CellAO comment, which read bit 3 as a suppression timer and level and bit 5 as faction standings, was right on both counts - and the client says so itself, through the localised text keyed TimeUntilGasChanges that the suppression pair is formatted into. The three names go to name slots 1, 2 and 4 through SetName''s own setter, the city playfield to the member N3Msg_GetCityNameForClanMember reads, the org rank to the member N3Msg_GetClanLevelString returns, the side experience to the member N3Msg_GetInfoPacketSideXpBonus returns, the land list to the one N3Msg_GetGridDestinationList returns, and the list that was called towers is an ACGItem list read by GameData''s own operator.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,1,'Flags','four combinations captured: 0x40, 0x41, 0x43 and 0x50','Bits, not a type. 0x01 an organization, 0x02 its city land, 0x04 towers, 0x08 suppression, 0x10 not a player, 0x20 twelve more int32s, 0x40 a version byte','proven'),
        @(30,1,'Version','1 in all 477 copies','Present only when the flags have 0x40; a zero here makes the client read one more byte and drop it','proven'),
        @('next',1,'Profession','0 for a monster, 1 to 15 for a player','The byte at the record + 0x32. The reader files it in the examined character''s stat table at 0x100463C5 with 0x3C - stat 60, profession. A byte here, though the profession enum elsewhere is int backed','proven'),
        @('next',1,'Level','1 in 490 of 532','The byte at + 0x33, filed with 0xA at 0x100463D3 - stat 10, professionlevel','proven'),
        @('next',1,'TitleLevel','1 in 527 of 532','The byte at + 0x34, filed with 0x25 at 0x100463E1 - stat 37, titlelevel','proven'),
        @('next','1 or nothing','Dropped byte','only when the version is zero','Read and not kept. Never on the wire, because nothing has sent a record without 0x40','proven'),
        @('next',1,'VisualProfession','tracks Profession in every captured copy','The record + 0x35, filed with 0x170 at 0x100463F2 - stat 368, visualprofession','proven'),
        @('next',2,'Side experience','29281, 5651, 340 and 500','The record + 0x30, and the client has an export for that member alone: N3Msg_GetInfoPacketSideXpBonus at 0x10027394 resolves the examined dynel, takes its InfoPacket at + 0x7C and returns the int16 at + 0x30. What a monster''s copy is for is still open, but the field is named by the client','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Health','99 distinct','The record + 0x20, filed with 0x1B at 0x100463FE - stat 27, health','proven'),
        @('next',4,'MaxHealth','90 distinct, and equal to Health in most copies','The record + 0x24, filed with 1 at 0x1004640A - stat 1, life','proven'),
        @('next',4,'BreedHostility','0, 100, 30 and -100','The inherited name is exactly right, and the client says so: the record + 0x28 is filed with 0xCC at 0x10046432 - stat 204, breedhostility','proven'),
        @('next',4,'OrganizationId','0, 9988 and 122884','Unconditional, which is the fix of 2026-09-11: two copies carry 9988 with no organization bit set','proven'),
        @('next','counted','FirstName','int16 length; Logan in the one named copy','The record + 0x48. The reader hands it to the name-slot setter at 0x1005BB92 with slot 1, at 0x100462FB - the same setter SetName drives, where slot 1 is the first name','proven'),
        @('next','counted','LastName','int16 length; Messamore in the one named copy','The record + 0x64, into name slot 2 at 0x10046336','proven'),
        @('next','counted','Auxiliary name','int16 length; empty in all 532','The record + 0x80, into name slot 4 at 0x10046382 - the auxiliary heap-backed dynel name, which SetName''s page has. Called LegacyTitle until 2026-09-12 on the inherited model''s word; the slot is what is established','proven'),
        @('next','counted','Display text','empty in all 477 copies','Not a name, which is why it is at the front of the record at + 4 rather than beside the three at + 0x48, + 0x64 and + 0x80: it is the examine window''s free text, and the client appends its own lines to whatever the server sent. The suppression line is the one that can be read whole - 0x1004651D appends the TimeUntilGasChanges text, gas percentage and time included, to exactly this string','proven'),
        @('next','counted','OrganizationRank','flags 0x01','The record + 0x9C, and the client has an export for exactly that member: N3Msg_GetClanLevelString at 0x10018EED resolves the examined dynel, takes its InfoPacket at + 0x7C and returns the std::string at + 0x9C. Unit Commander in the one copy that has a rank at all, which is what a clan level string is','proven'),
        @('next','an X3F1 list','Grid destinations','flags 0x01 and 0x02 together','The client''s own name for it: the list lives at the record + 0xB8 and the export that returns it is N3Msg_GetGridDestinationList, which at 0x10016CA5 resolves the examined dynel, takes its InfoPacket at + 0x7C and returns that member. It is the same record GridDestinationSelect carries, read by the same 0x1012905A - a playfield id, an Identity, a label and two reserved int32s - and that packet''s page has the field meanings. Two entries in the one captured copy, Stret River Island and Aprils Rock Offense, with 695 and 635 as the playfield ids; those looked wrong for a playfield only because they were being compared with 6010, which is a city instance','proven'),
        @('next',4,'CityPlayfieldId','6010; flags 0x01','Part of the 0x01 branch and not unconditional, which was the other half of the 2026-09-11 fix. The reader stores it at + 0xF0 of the character info object at 0x100463A1, and N3Msg_GetCityNameForClanMember reads exactly that member at 0x10016E39 and turns it into a name - so it is the playfield of the organisation''s city','proven'),
        @('next','an X3F1 list','ACG items','flags 0x04','Not towers: the list at the record + 0xCC is read at 0x100468E1 by GameData''s own operator for ACGItem_t, and N3Msg_GetInfoPacketACGItemData hands entries back one at a time by index. So the four int32s are an ACGItem - the two templates, the quality and the reserved word ACGItem''s reader consumes and discards, which KnuBotRejectedItems'' page already describes. The model''s duplicate Tower class is gone; it is AcgItem, the same record MailRecord attaches','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Suppression timer','flags 0x08','Seconds until the suppression gas changes. Never captured, but the client leaves no room: 0x1004646F divides the record + 0xD8 by 3600 and then by 60, and feeds the hours and minutes into the localised text keyed TimeUntilGasChanges at 0x100464A2. The finished line is appended to the record''s + 4, the examine window''s free text','proven'),
        @('next',1,'Suppression level','flags 0x08','Which gas, not how much time. The byte at the record + 0xDC is tested at 0x10046457: 1 gives 25, 2 gives 5 and anything else 75 - the three suppression gas percentages - and the number goes into the same TimeUntilGasChanges line','proven'),
        @('next',48,'Faction standings','flags 0x20','Twelve int32s, and the reader names every one of them: it files the record + 0xE0 through + 0x10C into the examined character''s stat table with 0x231 to 0x23C at 0x10046574 onward - stats 561 to 572, which are clansentinels, otmed, clangaia, ottrans, clanvanguards, gos, otfollowers, otoperator, otunredeemed, clandevoted, clanconserver and clanredeemed. The inherited comment called the flag hasFaction and guessed a standing per faction; that guess is now the client''s own word','proven'),
        @('next',4,'InvadersKilled','1234567890 for a monster, 15500 for the one player who has any','The first of the three unconditional int32s at 0x10046174, filed with 0x267 at 0x10046299 - stat 615, invaderskilled','proven'),
        @('next',4,'KilledByInvaders','1234567890 for a monster, 0 for a player','Filed with 0x268 at 0x100462B1 - stat 616, killedbyinvaders','proven'),
        @('next',4,'AlienLevel','1234567890 for a monster, 30 for the one player who has any','Filed with 0xA9 at 0x100462C9 - stat 169, alienlevel. GUI.dll prints it in the examine window under the label DefenderRank, at 0x10034FB4','proven'),
        @('next',32,'Eight PvP int32s','absent when flags 0x10 is set','The reader files them into the stat table one after another from 0x10046655: the record + 0x110 through + 0x12C with 0x2A2, 0x2A3, 0x2A4, 0x2A6, 0x2A8, 0x2AA, 0x2AB and 0x2AC - stats 674 pvpduelkills, 675 pvpdueldeaths, 676 pvpprofessionduelkills, 678 pvprankedsolokills, 680 pvprankedteamkills, 682 pvpsoloscore, 683 pvpteamscore and 684 pvpduelscore. Note the gaps in the ids and note the third: the inherited model called it a loss count and the client calls it kills','proven')
    )
}
$known['MarketSend'] = @{
    Status='complete'; Direction='client &rarr; server'; Length='45 + 8 per item, up to eight items'; Evidence='A deposit to the market: up to eight items and an amount of credits, and the amount is what kept this amber until 2026-09-11. Extracted-client MarketSendIIR_c has vtable 0x10159BCC, reader 0x1002D83D, writer 0x1002D65A and dispatcher 0x1002D5BA. The reader clears its vector, takes an Identity, an int32 and then an X3F1-counted list of Identities, and trims that list to eight entries afterwards at 0x1002D888 - so eight is the most that can arrive whatever the count says. The writer emits the same three in the same order. The constructor at 0x1002D787 is called from exactly one place, N3Msg_SendMarketItem(vector&lt;Identity_t&gt; const&amp;, int) at 0x1001E175, and that call settles two of the three: the Identity in the body is a copy of the message&#39;s own identity, which the sender takes from GetClientControlDynel, so it is the character doing the sending; and the list is the vector of item identities the function is handed. The dispatcher agrees - it resolves that Identity to a character and raises the feedback string &quot;Feedback_MarketItemSent&quot; - and then emits a global signal at GlobalSignals_c + 0x288 with no arguments at all, a bare notification, so the client never reads the int32 or the list back. That is why the int32 had no name: nothing on the receiving side touches it, and the signature it arrives in carries no parameter names. The name is on the sending side, in GUI.dll. Interfaces.dll re-exports N3Msg_SendMarketItem and GUI.dll imports it and calls it from exactly one place, at 0x10009DA5, inside the handler that begins at 0x10009C40 - the window whose own strings a few hundred bytes on are &quot;Deposit to Market&quot;, &quot;SendButton&quot;, &quot;ItemView_&quot; and Views/MarketSendWindow.xml. That handler reads the text field at its + 0x78, puts it through atoi, and takes zero when the field is empty. A negative amount is refused outright: it fetches LDB text 0x6E, which the string beside the call names Feedback_MarketNoNegative, pushes it at the chat signal GlobalSignals_c + 0x184 and returns without sending anything. What is left is then clamped - N3Msg_GetSkill(0x3D, 2), and 0x3D is 61, cash, with if (amount &gt; cash) amount = cash at 0x10009D2A - so the client will not offer more than the character has. So the int32 is an amount of credits. It is not an item&#39;s price: the guard at 0x10009D8E sends whenever there is either an item or an amount, so a deposit of pure credits goes out with an empty list, and the two are independent. No capture contains one. MarketSendCarriesCreditsWithOrWithoutItems covers both shapes, and before it the message had no C# at all.'
    Tests='MarketSendCarriesCreditsWithOrWithoutItems One thing the client does that a sender needs to know: it keeps at most eight entries. The reader works out the list length as (end less begin) over 8 at 0x1002D881 and, when that is above 8, resizes the vector down at 0x1002DA09. A ninth entry is read off the wire and then dropped.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,8,'Sender','the character making the deposit','A copy of the message&#39;s own identity, taken from GetClientControlDynel by the sender, and the character the dispatcher raises &quot;Feedback_MarketItemSent&quot; on','proven'),
        @(37,4,'Credits','clamped to stat 61','The second argument of N3Msg_SendMarketItem. GUI.dll 0x10009C40 atois the window&#39;s text field into it, refuses a negative with Feedback_MarketNoNegative, and clamps it to N3Msg_GetSkill(0x3D, 2) - stat 61, cash','proven'),
        @(41,4,'Item count','X3F1','The reader trims the list to eight entries afterwards regardless of this','proven'),
        @(45,'8 per item','Items','identities of the items being deposited; may be empty when only credits go in','The vector N3Msg_SendMarketItem is handed, built at GUI.dll 0x10009D57 by walking the window&#39;s slots and skipping the empty ones','proven')
    )
}

$known['SetName'] = @{
    Status='values'; Direction='server → client'; Length='50 plus the name'; Evidence='Three of the six fields are the client&#39;s own and the other three are read and dropped. Extracted-client SetNameIIR_t has vtable 0x101622F8, reader 0x10077090, writer 0x10077004 and dispatcher 0x10077207. The reader takes an int32, a byte, an int32 length and that many raw bytes, an Identity and a final int32; the writer emits the same six in the same order, so there are two independent sources for the layout. The only constructor, at 0x10077065, is reached from the message factory at 0x1000BDCD and from nowhere else, so the client never builds one of these and the reader and the dispatcher are all there is. The reader also sets the bounds: it refuses the whole message unless the length is at least 1 and below 0x20, so a name is one to thirty one bytes and carries no terminator on the wire - the NUL the reader writes at 0x10077100 goes into its own stack buffer. The dispatcher resolves the message&#39;s own identity with n3Dynel_t::GetDynel and calls 0x1005BB92 with the int32 and the string. That function is a four way switch on the int32 and each arm writes a different std::string on the dynel: 1 goes to +0x170, 2 to +0x18C, 3 to +0x154 and 4 to a heap string at +0x1A8, with anything else falling through to 0x1005BD4C, which does nothing. Two of the four are named outright by the exports that read those same members straight back - n3EngineClientAnarchy_t::N3Msg_GetFirstName returns +0x170 at 0x10018968 and N3Msg_GetLastName returns +0x18C at 0x100189A5. The constructor at 0x1005D13B initialises the three inline strings in the order +0x154, +0x170, +0x18C, which puts slot 3&#39;s string immediately in front of the first name. Slot 3 is the one Clone uses, and it is the only slot the client treats specially: the dispatcher clears bit 0x40 through two virtual calls before storing, and the store at 0x1005BCF6 walks the string replacing every &#39;/&#39; with an &#39;X&#39; - which is what a name a person typed gets, and not what a name the server owns needs. Naming slots 3 and 4 is what is left, and the byte, the Identity and the trailing int32 with them: the reader keeps the byte as a boolean at the message&#39;s +0x38 and the Identity and int32 at +0x3C and +0x44, the writer emits all three again, and the dispatcher reads none of them. No capture contains one, so SetNameMatchesTheLayoutTakenFromTheClient pins the layout rather than checking it against retail; before this the message had no C# and no write-up at all.'
    Tests='SetNameMatchesTheLayoutTakenFromTheClient The first captures of this message arrived on 2026-09-11, in a session with two accounts teaming; before that there were none. Three copies, and they do not settle the three unnamed fields - they corroborate what the client already said about them. The name slot is 3 in all three and the names are the two characters in the session, Dedeengi twice and Ricohelper once. The byte, the Identity and the int32 that the dispatcher never reads are zero and empty in all three, which is what a field the sender has nothing to put in looks like. What the captures do prove is the layout, which until now rested on the client&#39;s reader alone: all three round-trip byte for byte.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,4,'Name slot','1 to 4','1 first name; 2 last name; 3 main character name, proven by Clone and three retail packets; 4 auxiliary heap-backed dynel name. Any other value does nothing','proven'),
        @(33,1,'Reserved Boolean','0 in all three captures','Codec-preserved compatibility Boolean ignored by the dispatcher','proven'),
        @(34,4,'Name length','1 to 31','The reader refuses the whole message outside that range','proven'),
        @(38,'the length','Name','no terminator on the wire','Handed to 0x1005BB92 with the slot. For slot 3 every &#39;/&#39; becomes an &#39;X&#39; at 0x1005BCF6','proven'),
        @('next',8,'Reserved identity','0:0 in all three captures','Codec-preserved compatibility Identity ignored by the dispatcher','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Reserved int32','0 in all three captures','Codec-preserved compatibility word ignored by the dispatcher','proven')
    )
}

$known['QuestFullUpdate'] = @{
    Status='values'; Direction='server → client'; Length='34 plus the quests; 559, 559, 555 and 671 in the four captured copies'; Evidence='Two fields of its own, both settled, on top of a record that is not. Extracted-client QuestFullUpdateIIR_t has vtable 0x101683E8, reader 0x100ACF68, writer 0x100ACFA2 and dispatcher 0x100ACFDE. The reader hands the stream to the quest list reader at 0x100AC2BA and then takes one byte, compares it against zero and keeps the answer as a bool at the message&#39;s +0x28; the writer emits the list and that byte. The list reader is a plain X3F1 count - it divides by 0x3F1 and refuses a remainder - and then, per entry, an Identity, a 0xF8 byte quest object constructed from it at 0x100AB79A, and the rest of the record read by 0x100ABEA7. That record is the same one QuestAlternative carries, and it was walked to the last instruction on 2026-09-11 - reward box, version-gated tail and all - so what keeps this off green is no longer the shape but the handful of fields inside it that have no meaning yet. The byte is settled, and it is not what this repository called it. The dispatcher resolves the message identity, asks the character for its quest list, and then calls 0x10056CDD once per quest in the list with the byte as its second argument. That function appends the quest to the character&#39;s list and then, only when the byte is non-zero and the character carries the +0x140 flag that marks the one the player controls, raises the feedback string "Feedback_YouGotANewMission" and emits GlobalSignals_c + 0x154 with the quest&#39;s identity. So it is an announcement flag - per send, not per quest - and it was modelled as HasMore, a continuation marker, which it is not. Nothing in any shipped image subscribes to + 0x154; SignalMap.py finds the emit and nothing else. Retail settles which way it sets the byte, and it agrees. The capture taken on 2026-09-11 of a character made from scratch carries four copies, each with one quest. The quest &quot;Kill a Leet&quot; appears in two of them: in the first zone session, at the point it was given, with the byte set; and again near the start of the next zone session, the window refresh after zoning, with the byte clear. The other two are quests given during that second session and both carry it set. So the byte marks the send that gives the quest and is clear on a refresh, which is what this server now does - it used to set it on every send, including the refresh on entering a playfield. All four round trip byte for byte. Two of QuestInfo&#39;s unnamed int32s are a time limit, found on 2026-09-11. Across all 193 captured quest records the pair holds the same value every time - 185 pairs of zero, four of 2880 and four of 7200 - and two independent int32s do not do that. 2880 seconds is forty-eight minutes and 7200 is two hours, which are mission time limits and not much else; zero is a quest without one. Why there are two is not settled: a limit beside a remaining time would be equal at the moment a mission is granted, and every captured record is a zone-in snapshot rather than a mid-mission one, so the captures cannot separate them. The client&#39;s own N3Msg_QuestRemainingTime reads neither - it walks the quest&#39;s action list and takes a deadline off QuestAction_t + 0x5C, a different field in a different record, which is a lead for the action record rather than this one. Some of the client&#39;s own member map came out of the same trace and is worth carrying even though it does not name a field on its own. The per-quest reader at Gamecode 0x100ABEA7 takes an int32 version and abandons the record unless it is 7 to 15; then two int32s into one stack local, which is what makes them unread; then the flags into Quest_t + 0xA4, the short string into + 8 and the long string into + 0x88, all three matching the exported accessors. Then an Identity into + 0x38 - the quest giver - a reward box through the reader at 0x100870A8, which has a version gate of its own at 3 to 6, an Identity into + 0x40, and int32s into + 0x48, + 0x70 and + 0x74 before the action list at + 0x28. N3Msg_GetQuestIcon returns + 0x48, and walking the reward box settled which member that is. The box, at Quest_t + 0x34 and read by 0x100870A8, has a version of its own gated 3 to 6 and most of its body is conditional on it: always the cash into box + 0, an int32 into a discarded stack local, the experience into box + 4, two X3F1 Identity lists and an item list at box + 0x1C; then three more int32s at box + 8, + 0x14 and + 0x18 only from version 4, two more at box + 0xC and + 0x10 only from version 5, and a whole GameData::ACGItem_t at box + 0x28 only from version 6, read through the client&#39;s own stream operator for that type. N3Msg_QuestGetCashReward and N3Msg_QuestGetXPReward confirm the first two - both resolve the quest, take + 0x34 and then + 0 and + 4 - and N3Msg_GetItemRewardList takes box + 0x28 and compares it against GameData::ACGItem_t::zero. So the box swallows nine of the model&#39;s members, 8 through 23, and with that counted the rest falls out: the Identity after it at + 0x40 is Owner, and the int32 after that at + 0x48 is MissionIconId, which is therefore the quest icon by the client&#39;s own accessor rather than by the shape of its values. The two seconds fields land at + 0x70 and + 0x74, and the action list at + 0x28 follows them. The naive alignment, before the box was walked, put the icon three members earlier on a field that is zero in all 193 records - which would have meant a client that never draws one. That is what the off-by-one was. One thing this exposes rather than fixes: the model flattens the box&#39;s version-gated tail as unconditional quest fields. Every captured record carries box version 6, so all four branches are taken and the flattened read is byte-exact, but a server sending version 3 would put none of members 15 to 23 on the wire and the model would read past the end. Members 20 to 23 are also not four loose int32s - they are one ACGItem_t, the same record the model already has an AcgItem class for. The action record was mapped the same way on 2026-09-11 and gives up two more names. Each action is 0x8C bytes, read by 0x100ACBA0: an int32 version, then five Identities, four floats, an Identity, four more floats, an Identity, two int32s, an Identity, and a GameData::WorldPos_c. The first of the two int32s, at QuestAction_t + 0x5C, is a deadline. N3Msg_QuestRemainingTime - exported from Interfaces.dll at 0x1000A8AC - is the whole argument: it walks the quest&#39;s action list, takes each action&#39;s + 0x5C, skips the zeros and returns the difference against the server clock, and a deadline is the only thing you subtract a clock from. The values agree: eight of the 193 captured records carry a non-zero one and they decode as 2026-09-13 01:09 and 01:26 and 2026-09-16 12:00, 12:04 and 12:05, which is one day and five days after the captures they arrived in. The other 185 are zero. Its old name in the model was UnknownHash15, which is what a timestamp looks like if you do not check the date. The tail is one record rather than six loose fields: the reader hands + 0x6C straight to the client&#39;s own stream operator for GameData::WorldPos_c, and N3Msg_GetQuestWorldPos at 0x1001ABC9 reaches the quest&#39;s action list, takes the first action, adds 0x6C and returns the Identity at + 0 with vectors from + 8 and + 0x14. That is what an earlier note meant when it said GetQuestWorldPos reaches the marker through the action list rather than off the quest - the marker is this field. One loose end from that trace is closed. The WorldPos_c is 32 bytes in memory and 28 on the wire, which looked like the model reading four bytes short in a list record - the kind of mistake that shifts every element after the first and that a byte-exact round trip would not catch on a one-element list. It is not one. GameData.dll&#39;s reader at 0x1000CA52 reads an Identity, two int32s and three floats - 28 bytes - and then builds the second Vector3 rather than reading it: each int32 goes through fild into components 0 and 2 and an fldz goes into component 1, so the derived position has no height. N3Msg_GetQuestWorldPos hands its caller both vectors, the read one and the derived one. The three floats are the marker and the captures say so independently: X runs 3431 to 3621 and Z 797 to 880, the same magnitude as the character coordinates in the SimpleCharFullUpdates of the same sessions, and Y is zero in 192 of 193. The int pair is not the same position in the same units - 100000 with 100000 in 173 records, which reads as a sentinel, and otherwise 41975 with 45030 and 34495 with 29814. The reward box stopped being flattened on 2026-09-11. Its version-gated tail - three int32s from version 4, two more from version 5, an ACGItem_t from version 6 - was modelled as nine unconditional members, which is byte-exact on every captured record because every captured record is version 6, and would read past the end of one that was not. The gate at 0x10087117 refuses anything outside 3 to 6, so the conditions can be written as exact value lists rather than comparisons and mean the same thing. The four loose int32s at the end became the one ACGItem_t they always were. All 241 records still round trip byte for byte. Two of the box&#39;s fields have outgrown their own notes. Both were recorded as zero in all 193 records; the corpus is 241 and 73 of them carry values. Box + 0xC holds a second four character code - 925D, 5UFZ, XX76, N6MO, 58YD, IGMR, E7AC, 12Y8, MMSU, L5H7, 8YIR, QSOR, 3JRQ, DIRM, MSRE, YNRL and IWQB, every one of the seventeen printable, which is the shape of a mission key - and box + 0x10 holds 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 9, 10, 11, 39, 212 or 250 on the same 73 records. The obvious route was tried on 2026-09-12 and closed: SimpleCharFullUpdate''s weapon pair carries four character codes too and every one of those is in the resource database, beside the Equip_ record it belongs to. None of these seventeen is. That is not a gap in the search but the answer to a different question - a mission the content generator built has no authored record to be named from, so a key for one can only have been made when the mission was, and it will not be found by reading the client. They move together. Which kind of quest carries them is not settled: splitting the captures by quest name does not separate them, but a packet carries up to three quests and that test could not say which record inside a packet the code belonged to. And one thing is now ruled out rather than merely unclaimed - box + 8 and box + 0x14 are consecutive on the wire and were read as the two halves of an Identity, but the reader puts them eight bytes apart with two other fields between, and an Identity is read as a unit. The tail was walked instruction by instruction on 2026-09-11, and the last ten members of the quest record turn out to be version-gated exactly as the reward box is. From 0x100AC08C the reader is a staircase - jge against 8, then 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 and 15, each else arm zeroing its member instead of reading it - and this model read all ten unconditionally. Every captured record is version 15, so every arm is taken and all 241 are byte exact; a server sending 14 would have left four bytes on the wire, 13 a whole list, and so on down. The gate at 0x100ABECE refuses any version outside 7 to 15, so the value lists that express it are exact and not an approximation. One arm gives the pattern away: below version 8 the client does not zero that member, it writes 6 - and our own server already sends 6 there, from a constant named for the field, which is the client&#39;s default arrived at independently. Three shapes in the middle of the tail came out of the same walk. The two int32 lists are plain int32 counts rather than X3F1, and every element is masked with 0x07FFFFFF before the client keeps it, so the top five bits of each are on the wire and discarded. The character info list is an int32 count and then, per entry, an Identity, an int32 length and that many bytes - which the client reads into a stack buffer and does nothing with. And a warning for the next person: 0x100458BB, which appears all through this tail, is not a stream reader. It takes a member vector and a value and appends, so a trace that reads the call listing rather than the instructions will count list readers that are not there. That mistake was made first and corrected here.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,4,'Quest count','(N + 1) * 0x3F1','The reader divides by 0x3F1 and refuses a remainder','proven'),
        @(33,'per record','Quests','241 records captured','An Identity and then the record read by 0x100ABEA7. The shape is confirmed rather than asserted: QuestConsume.py decodes these messages with the client&#39;s reader written out as code, knowing nothing about the C# model, and all 32 captured copies consume to the byte - the last field of the last quest ends exactly where the message does. It found one error doing it, the version 15 block&#39;s count being an X3F1 sentinel where the version 13 block&#39;s four fields earlier is a plain int32. The four-character values that looked like a misplaced boundary are field values: they land on the reward box&#39;s version 4 and version 5 fields, and across the captures there are dozens of them - OTGD, QSOR, 2OGZ, 3JRQ, R2GL, ALHA, LE01 - all in the uppercase-and-digit alphabet an ACGHash_t uses. What is left unnamed is meaning, not shape: two int32s near the front, those two hashes and the value beside them, and most of the version-gated tail','values'),
        @('next',1,'Announce as new','0 or 1','Non-zero raises &quot;Feedback_YouGotANewMission&quot; once per quest in the list, for the character the player controls','proven')
    )
}

$known['QuestAlternative'] = @{
    Tests='QuestAlternativeHeaderIsTwentyTwoBytesInTheClientsOrder, QuestOriginatorPairsEachBoothWithItsTeamVersion'
    Status='values'; Direction='both'; Length='51 plus the missions'; Evidence='Every byte is read and all but one are named. This page did not exist until 2026-09-12 - the packet had a managed schema and no byte table - and the first cut of it, written the same day, was wrong twice and is worth saying so. It claimed no capture contains one; four do, each carrying five missions, and all four round trip byte for byte. And it left two rows open that the schema had already named, because the schema was not read first. Both names come from one error message, which the first pass had not found: at 0x1016BB20 the client prints &quot;ERROR: invalid data for ACGQuestIIR_t %u:%u in stream: Difficulty = %u (valid is [%d, %d]) / Dimension values = %s / Seed = %u / Originator = %u:%u %u (valid is [%d, %d])&quot; and the pushes at 0x100CA062 line those four names up with the fields the reader filled. So the int32 is a seed and the byte with the identity after it is an originator. Extracted-client QuestAlternativeIIR_t has vtable 0x1016C004, reader 0x100CB232, dispatcher 0x100CB181 and version 0x100CB4B4, and it derives from ACGQuestIIR_t, which is abstract - three of that base''s slots are _purecall - so the base''s own id is not a message and its page says so. The reader is two parts: 0x100CB24A calls the base reader at 0x100C9F49 for the first twenty one bytes, then a count and that many missions. It is the mission terminal, and it goes both ways, which is the one thing the first pass got right that nothing else had recorded. The client builds one of these itself: n3EngineClientAnarchy_t::N3Msg_GenerateMissions at 0x10016276 takes a MissionGenerateInfo_t, constructs a QuestAlternativeIIR_t at 0x100CB351 - which is where the name string at 0x1016BA64 comes from - fills in the difficulty, the six dimensions and the originator pair, and hands it to n3EngineClient_t::SendIIRToServer. The server answers with the same shape carrying up to five missions, and the dispatcher walks them into the list the mission window reads through N3InterfaceModule_t::N3Msg_GetMissionSelectionList; that window is the one that says &quot;No missions available, please try again later!&quot; when the list comes back empty. So a server has to accept this message as a request and answer it with the same id. Three further things GameData names rather than the captures. The six bytes are a GameData::DimensionValues_t, read one byte per dimension at 0x100022FD, and DimensionValues_t::GetDimensionName at 0x1000219B is a table of the six in wire order. They are signed: the operator adds a hundred to each and refuses anything over two hundred, which is why the captured 229 and 254 are in range - they are minus 27 and minus 2. The originator is a nine value enum and GetQuestOriginatorName at GameData.dll 0x10002D0A is a table of its names, with IsTeamOriginator at 0x10002D23 answering yes to the even ones, which pairs each booth with its team version; all four captures carry NeutralBooth. And the mission count is capped by the client''s other complaint, 0x100CB27C: &quot;ERROR: invalid data in QuestAlternativeIIR_t stream %u:%u: - Number of quests = %u&quot;, printed when it is above five. The mission record itself needs no work: it is the same 0xF8 byte quest record QuestFullUpdate carries, read by the same 0x100ABEA7, and that page walks it to the last instruction. The byte after each mission was the last thing outstanding and it is now a dead end rather than a question. It travels with its mission into the sixteen byte MissionData_t records the mission window reads, GUI.dll copies each record whole into a MissionListEntry_c, and from there nothing reads it: not the window, and not the one path that talks back to the server, since N3Msg_SelectMission carries only the mission''s Identity out of the record. So every row here is proven and what keeps the packet amber is the same thing that keeps sixteen others amber - two of its fields are ones the client reads and drops. Across the four captures the five bytes read 0, 1, 2, then 3 or 6, then 6, 9 or 24, which is index-like at the front and is not an index at the back; recorded in case a capture of a terminal offering fewer than five ever separates them. What a roll comes back with was measured on 2026-09-25, and the answer has a shape. Twenty one rolls were expanded through this project&#39;s own serializer - the four in 20260910-200346 stream 2, which carry four different dimension settings, and the seventeen in 20260923-201746 stream 8, where the owner reversed every slider between sets on purpose. Every one of the twenty one answers is three missions of one type followed by one each of two others, in that order. That is the shape the community slider tables have always described and it had never been checked here. The type is the mission&#39;s MissionIconId, and five of its values now have names taken from the assignment text each offer carries, which is formulaic enough to separate them cleanly: 11329 return item, 11330 kill, 11335 find person, 11337 find item, 11342 repair. Three of those agree with the type codes read out of the quest record of a mission that was entered, found separately and earlier. The choice is deterministic in the six dimensions and in nothing else: fifteen consecutive rolls at one setting returned the same three types with seeds running from 180241537 to 2141450657, and six of the fifteen went out at difficulty 11 and nine at difficulty 1, so neither the seed nor the difficulty touches it. Which dimension picks which type is not settled and is not guessed - six settings against six dimensions does not determine it - but because the answer is deterministic one roll per setting is enough to map the whole function, and the six that have been measured are written up in Documentation/Missions.md.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,1,'Format version','4 in all four captured copies','Read by the base at 0x100C9F87 and compared with virtual slot 9, QuestAlternativeIIR_t::0x100CB4B4. A mismatch prints &quot;Can''t read ACGQuestIIR_t %u:%u from stream: Invalid version %u, current is %u&quot; at 0x100C9FB3 and the packet is dropped','proven'),
        @(30,1,'Difficulty','6, 9 and 11 in the four captures','Named by the client''s own error, which calls it the difficulty and prints its bounds. The reader takes value minus one and refuses anything above ten, so 1 to 11 is what it accepts. The request side agrees: N3Msg_GenerateMissions at 0x10016276 takes the nought to a hundred slider at MissionGenerateInfo_t + 0, folds it with floor(value * 0.10891088843345642 + 1.0) and passes the result to the setter at 0x100C9EB5, which writes the member this reader fills','proven'),
        @(31,6,'Mission dimensions','signed, -100 to 100','A GameData::DimensionValues_t, read a byte at a time by GameData.dll''s own operator at 0x100022FD, which adds a hundred to each and refuses anything over two hundred - so they are signed, and the captured 229 and 254 are minus 27 and minus 2. DimensionValues_t::GetDimensionName at 0x1000219B names all six in this order: good vs bad, controlled vs lacking control, open vs hidden, physical vs mystical, explosive vs patient, money reward vs experience reward - the mission terminal''s sliders. The request carries the same six, set into a DimensionValues_t at indices 0 to 5 from MissionGenerateInfo_t + 4 onwards','proven'),
        @(37,4,'Seed','four distinct values in four copies','Named by the client''s error message, which prints it as the seed. The dispatcher at 0x100CB1D9 copies it unchanged into every mission record it builds for the window, so it is one value for the answer rather than one per mission - which is what a generator seed for a batch of missions would be. Not in the request: nothing in N3Msg_GenerateMissions writes it','proven'),
        @(41,1,'Originator','NeutralBooth in all four captures','Where the missions came from, and the client names all nine: GetQuestOriginatorName at GameData.dll 0x10002D0A is a table of them and IsTeamOriginator at 0x10002D23 answers yes to the even ones, pairing each booth with its team version - unknown, neutral booth, neutral booth team, Omni-Tek booth and team, clan booth and team, NPC and NPC team. The reader refuses anything above 8 and prints the bound. It is not a loose byte: the client''s only setter for it, 0x100C9EC6, takes it together with the identity below and writes neither unless this is in range and the identity is non-zero','proven'),
        @(42,8,'Mission terminal','type 56001 in all four captures','The terminal the missions came from, read at 0x100C9FF4 into + 0x3C and printed beside the originator in the same error line. The client fills it from MissionGenerateInfo_t + 0x20 through the setter above, and the dispatcher passes the pair on when it emits GlobalSignals_c + 0x164','proven'),
        @(50,1,'Number of missions','5 in all four captures','Named by the client''s own error: above five, 0x100CB27C prints &quot;ERROR: invalid data in QuestAlternativeIIR_t stream %u:%u: - Number of quests = %u&quot; and the packet is dropped. Zero is what the client sends on the way out, when it is asking rather than answering','proven'),
        @('next','a quest record plus 1, per mission','Missions','five per captured copy','Per entry the same 0xF8 byte quest record QuestFullUpdate carries - its leading Identity, then the body read by 0x100ABEA7, walked instruction by instruction on that page - and then one byte','proven'),
        @('within each','1','Trailing byte','0, 1, 2, then 3 or 6, then 6, 9 or 24','Followed to the end on 2026-09-12, and the end is a dead one. The reader puts it in the vector at + 0x70 while the mission goes into the one at + 0x60; the dispatcher walks the two together at 0x100CB1B8 and pairs each byte with its mission and with the seed into the sixteen byte MissionData_t records 0x10056EDD hands the mission window. The window reads them through N3Msg_GetMissionSelectionList and GUI.dll 0x100D2A38 copies each record whole into a MissionListEntry_c at + 0x120, so this byte lands at that view''s + 0x128. Nothing in the window''s code region reads it back, and the one path that sends anything to the server does not carry it: choosing a mission hands the whole record to N3Msg_SelectMission at 0x100D0DA5, and Gamecode''s implementation at 0x100173F5 builds its message through 0x100CB00C, which takes the record''s + 0 and + 4 - the mission''s Identity - and nothing else. So the byte reaches the interface and stops there, like the seed beside it. The values are index-like across the first three entries of every capture and stop being so for the last two, which is worth recording and is not a meaning','proven')
    )
}

$known['AcgQuest'] = @{
    Status='absent'; Direction='server → client'; Length='the front of a QuestAlternative'; Evidence='Not a message. The id hashes to a real class - ACGQuestIIR_t, vtable 0x1016BA80, reader 0x100C9F49, writer 0x100CA0F1 - which is why the class name scan reports it as carried, but the client cannot receive one and never could. Three of the vtable&#39;s slots are MSVCR100&#39;s _purecall through the thunk at 0x1013F1E8: slot 2, which is where every other message keeps its dispatcher, slot 5, and slot 9, which the reader itself calls at 0x100C9F8E to get the version to check against. A class with pure virtuals cannot be instantiated, so nothing ever constructs one of these and a copy arriving on the wire would reach _purecall and abort. ClassHierarchy.py says what it is instead: ACGQuestIIR_t has exactly one descendant, QuestAlternativeIIR_t at vtable 0x1016C004, and that one fills in all three slots - dispatcher 0x100CB181, 0x1013047C and version 0x100CB4B4. This is the abstract base of QuestAlternative, and its reader is the base portion QuestAlternative&#39;s own reader calls first, at 0x100CB24A, before reading its five further fields. The layout below is that base portion, recorded here because it is the front of every QuestAlternative and had not been written down. In order: a version byte, compared against the derived class&#39;s own and rejected with the printf at 0x100C9FB3 - "Can&#39;t read ACGQuestIIR_t %u:%u from stream: Invalid version %u, current is %u" - then a byte the reader refuses outside 1 to 11, a GameData::DimensionValues_t through that module&#39;s own operator, an int32, a byte refused outside 1 to 8, and an Identity refused when both halves are zero. The reader also refuses the whole thing at 0x100C9F64 unless the message&#39;s own identity is type 0xC350 with a non-zero instance. Nothing needs writing for this id. QuestAlternative, which is what needs writing, was written on 2026-09-12 and carries the layout below as its first twenty one bytes.'
    Fields=@(
        @(29,1,'Version','checked against the derived class&#39;s own','Rejected with a printf naming both versions','proven'),
        @(30,1,'Unnamed byte','1 to 11','The reader refuses anything outside that range','unresolved'),
        @('next','a DimensionValues_t','Dimension values','','Read by GameData&#39;s own operator through the import at 0x101546D4','proven'),
        @('next',4,'Unnamed int32','','','unresolved'),
        @('next',1,'Unnamed byte','1 to 8','The reader refuses anything outside that range','unresolved'),
        @('next',8,'Identity','never both halves zero','','unresolved')
